The hourly budget for new `{{ labels.element_type }}` elements is **nearly saturated**. When the budget is saturated, the receiver drops new elements of that type for the rest of the hour, which shows up as missing topology.

Each budget is a value per agent multiplied by the number of agents, so it grows when you add agents.

### Check for limited agents
The sizing profile limits how many nodes you can observe. Agents above that limit are marked as limited. The receiver rejects their process, container and connection data, and they do not add to the budgets. Raising a budget does not help limited agents.

1. Run `sts agent list`. The last line shows how many agents are active, limited and stale.
2. If any agents are limited, move to the next sizing profile. A profile change can change volume sizes and the Kafka partition count, so it takes more than a `helm upgrade`.

### Raise the budget per agent
If no agents are limited, the agents create more new elements per hour than the default budget per agent allows, for example because they run many short-lived pods.

1. Find the variable for the saturated element type:

| element type | environment variable |
|--------------|----------------------|
| component    | `CONFIG_FORCE_stackstate_receiver_processAgent_maxNewComponentsHourlyPerHost` |
| relation     | `CONFIG_FORCE_stackstate_receiver_processAgent_maxNewRelationsHourlyPerHost` |

2. Raise the value by 50% in your `values.yaml`, for example:
```
stackstate:
  components:
    receiver:
      extraEnv:
        open:
          CONFIG_FORCE_stackstate_receiver_processAgent_maxNewComponentsHourlyPerHost: "150"
```
On HA profiles, set the variable under `stackstate.components.receiver.split.processAgent.extraEnv.open`.

3. Redeploy via a `helm upgrade` including any other values files or extra settings you applied previously
```
helm upgrade \
--install \
--namespace suse-observability \
--values values.yaml \
suse-observability \
suse-observability/suse-observability
```

A higher budget increases the load on Correlate and Topology Sync, so watch their latency afterwards.

For more details, see [Receiver element budget saturated](https://documentation.suse.com/cloudnative/suse-observability/latest/en/setup/install-stackstate/data-ingestion-pipeline.html#_receiver_element_budget_saturated).
